title: The Meeting
~~~~

John: Let me say the quiet part out loud. After twenty years, I no longer believe students learn. They pass. They finish. They repeat the
  phrases we drill into them. But learn? Not the way you and I mean the word. Not anymore. You want them writing... what did you call it, Piotr?

Piotr: Mermaid.

John: *Mermaid.* You want them writing Mermaid. These are the students who put periods in the middle of sentences because their thumbs hit the wrong key on Instagram. The Instagram generation does not — cannot — sit with structured notation.

Piotr: John. The Instagram generation writes more markup before lunch than you have written in your career. [face=driven]

John: Excuse me?

Piotr: Hashtags. @-mentions. Bullet lists in captions. Emoji as semantic tokens. They format. They tag. They nest. They do it on a phone, with their thumbs, while walking. You want to tell me these kids can't type `graph LR` and an arrow?

John: That is not the same thing.

Piotr: It is exactly the same thing. It is a small grammar with strict rules and immediate visual feedback. That is the easiest possible learning environment. You type a wrong character, the picture breaks, you fix it. This is how children learn to talk. Except we are doing it with arrows.

John: *(smiling, the smile of a man about to cite something)* There's a wonderful piece by Wineburg —

Piotr: I have read it. It does not say what you think it says.

John: *(stiffening)* That is a fairly aggressive way to —

Piotr: John. Listen. This is not academic. Mermaid -- and similar formats -- live in the design documentation for this very system. Every README. Every architecture doc. Every postmortem. They are in the specs the new hire is handed on her first day at the consulting firm. They are in the slide deck the product manager glues together at midnight. They are in the documentation of every engineering team that has shipped anything this decade.

Piotr: A small grammar with strict rules and immediate visual feedback — *this is what college should be teaching*. Not as enrichment. As the core skill. The most generalizable thing we can hand a person is the ability to sit with a small notation, get one character wrong, see it break, fix it, and move on. Every white-collar job they will walk into next year asks them to do exactly that — with a different small notation each time.

Piotr: The students who never learn to sit with one will be the students for whom one more door closes quietly behind them. They will not even hear it close. *That* is what I am trying to prevent.

--- pause ---

Omer: *(carefully)* I — okay. Can I — can I jump in for a second? Because these are my students. And I think we may be — uh — talking past the actual question a little.

Piotr: Please.

Omer: So. The reason I brought this up at all is — you know I've been looking for concept-mapping tools. For the interdisciplinary course. The whole point of that class is helping students see that one problem — say, a kid who can't focus in school because their family is in crisis — touches economics, and psychology, and sociology, and policy. And right now I am doing that on a whiteboard. With markers. And it does not... travel. The students nod in class and then on the exam they write five paragraphs about psychology and forget the rest exists.

John: Which is exactly my point —

Omer: *(gently, not letting him in)* — which is why I want a tool. I want them to make the map. Not look at mine. Make their own. Because the making is the learning. That part I am sure about.

Piotr: Yes. Thank you. This is the entire argument. [face=smile]

Omer: *(to John)* And John, I hear you, I do, about the reading levels. I see those students. I have them in office hours. But — Piotr is not entirely wrong that they already do — uh — structured things. On their phones. They do.

John: Omer, with respect, I think you're being generous.

Omer: I think I'm being — *accurate*? Maybe? I — honestly, I don't know. That's why I want to try it.

Piotr: Try it.

Omer: Try it. Small. Very small. I'll offer it to a handful of students — extra credit, or just for fun, no pressure — and we give them the simplest possible thing. A cause. Two disciplines. An arrow from one to the other. That is the entire assignment. If three students do it and it's a disaster, I have learned something. If three students do it and it works, I have learned something else.

--- pause ---

John: *(after a beat)* ...That is actually a reasonable design.

Piotr: *(deadpan)* Yes John. Omer is reasonable. This is news.

Omer: *(laughing despite himself)* Piotr —

John: I'll allow it. As an experiment. With the understanding that I expect the results to —

Piotr: — confirm everything you already believe. Yes. We know.

Omer: *(quickly, before this restarts)* So we're doing it? All three of us? We're doing it.

Piotr: We are doing it.

John: *(sighing the sigh of a man humoring his colleagues)* We are doing it.

Omer: Okay. Okay! Good. I'll — I'll write to a few students this week. Something fun. Low stakes. "Draw me a picture of why your classmate can't focus in algebra, but in arrows."

Piotr: Send me what they make.

John: Send me what they make as well.

Omer: *(smiling, a little surprised it came together)* I will send you both what they make.
